 The source is [*Computational Complexity, Phase Transitions, and Message-Passing for Community Detection*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.2290). Section 5.1 defines the stochastic block model and asks for vertex types from the graph; Section 5.2 says that, for two groups, the second adjacency eigenvector separates the groups by sign. Literal SBM recovery is not an exact witness problem—the paper itself notes that a realized graph need not contain all information about the hidden types—so this generator uses an exact equitable specialization of the same graph/eigenvector object.
 
-An instance is a cyclic two-lift of a complete graph. The solver receives every lift twist and must return a normalized `+1/-1` vector. `verify` rebuilds the graph and checks the antipodal fibre rule and `A*x = lambda*x` using integer addition only. It never reads `inst["answer"]`. The generator samples the switching gauge first and builds all twist rows around it, so it knows the witness without recovering it from the graph.
+An instance is a cyclic two-lift of a complete graph. The solver receives every lift twist and a target integer eigenvalue, then must return a normalized `+1/-1` adjacency eigenvector. `verify` rebuilds the graph and checks the antipodal fibre rule and `A*x = lambda*x` using integer addition only. It never reads `inst["answer"]`. The generator samples the switching gauge first and builds all twist rows around it, so it knows the witness without recovering it from the graph.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-This is deliberately in the dense regular easy regime, not a Track-A claim. Section 5.2 gives the mechanical adjacency-spectral algorithm; the module's projected power iteration runs in `O(T(|V|+|E|))`, solved 8/8 hard instances, and averaged **56,001 arithmetic operations / 0.0145 s** in the recorded self-test. By contrast, once one notices the odd-cycle invariant, XORing one offset row reveals its unswitched sign and propagation recovers the gauge in **281 exact bit/sign operations**. The benchmark tests recognition of that invariant without a numerical eigensolver. The paper's sparse `O(1)`-degree warning—adjacency eigenvectors localize on high-degree structures—is avoided; that is the regime where the paper motivates the non-backtracking matrix.
