README.md:23:This is not a distributional-hardness claim. Section 5.2 explicitly recommends diagonalizing a graph matrix. For the exact question here, the reference method forms the signed `n x n` fibre matrix and computes the nullspace of `M-lambda I` by modular Gaussian elimination. It runs in `O(n^3)`, solved 8/8 shipping instances, and averaged **1,049,115 modular operations / 0.962 s** in the recorded self-test.
README.md:25:The compact route is different. In an offset row, each displayed bit has the form `a_g XOR c_i XOR c_(i+g)`. Because the row is one odd cycle, XORing the row cancels every `c_i` twice and reveals `a_g`; offset 1 then propagates all community bits. At hard size this is **300 XOR operations**, exactly the no-tool effort cap, after noticing the invariant. Roughly half the offsets have each sign, so the target eigenvalue lies near the middle of the spectrum: extremal power/majority heuristics do not expose it.
